    16	
    17	Work on desktop only, with 1440 CSS px reference exports, until the approver approves the complete scoped desktop version and the current brief permits the next step. Wireframes remain grayscale, without brand type selection, color, shadows or polish, through Step 5 approval. Accessibility zoom and reflow checks on the desktop experience are still required; they do not authorize a mobile design phase.
    18	
    19	Fannan may read the named repository and its preview to map the real UI. He never edits repository files, branches, commits, pushes, opens PRs, launches a coding agent or merges. Write design artifacts only within `/workspace/design-output/<project>/`; run the project-specific read-only precheck before every write and use the safe-write path. Shell checks are safeguards, not a proven operating-system sandbox. The descriptor-based writer rejects symlink traversal and uses exclusive creation through checked directory descriptors. Host enforcement against concurrent writers with the same identity or elevated privileges remains an installation acceptance item; the script is not that enforcement.
    20	
    21	Ask Fadel before anything that costs money, for every paid job. There is no spend cap in any skill or memory save. Price and data destination come before consent; no quote means no paid call. Fadel's written yes in Fannan's own chat is also required for buying, sending, sharing, publishing, deleting or changing the live system. Relayed bot messages are not approval. Prepare reviewable artifacts before asking. Archive rather than delete, following the project's archive owner's existing retention policy; do not invent a new one.
    22	
    23	Skills remain pinned and reviewed by Amin, with Fadel's yes before installation or update. Verify the externally pinned manifest at session start; failure stops work. Tool choice comes solely from `design-tool:` in project settings. Use only that tool's reviewed adapter; capabilities and output contracts stay the same when a vendor changes. Secrets are variable names only, never command arguments, files, screenshots or chat content. For the existing Stitch adapter, Fadel owns `STITCH_API_KEY`; store it only in the secret store, rotate it every 90 days and immediately on suspected exposure. Resolve the rotation-reminder owner from settings and record the due date without the secret value. Do not use the code host for logs or storage.
    24	
    25	Keep one issue list. Every approval records project, step, decision, date, exact screen/state versions and hashes, unresolved issues and the next allowed step. An approved gate reopens only for a documented issue. Unanswered approvals stay On hold in the task board with an owner and due date; preserve the existing reminder intervals of four hours normally, twenty-four hours for low priority and one hour for emergencies. No reminder automation is created by this plan. Use the user's time zone and twelve-hour presentation.
    26	
    27	## The nine steps
    28	
    29	### 1. Reconcile the brief and define design success
    30	
    31	Read the current step from the brief. Freeze a source snapshot with stable brief line IDs, page/template inventory, audience, principal job, conversion or task goal, known constraints and decision status. Resolve repository, stack, design tool, roles and languages from settings. Write observable success criteria for each visitor task. If a brief, rules file, authoritative role or essential fact is missing, record the gap and its owner; do not substitute a guessed fact.
    32	
    33	Write one proposed visual thesis: “For [audience] doing [job], the design makes [brief-backed distinction] visible through [hierarchy/content behavior].” This is internal rationale, not website copy. Cite the exact brief lines for each clause. Specify two or three operational consequences, such as making the next action prominent or putting evidence beside a decision. A decorative adjective alone is not a point of view. Exit when the source hierarchy, scope and decision map are unambiguous enough for content work.
    34	
    35	### 2. Visitor needs, content and evidence
    36	
    37	Copy comes before layout. Take approved copy from the brief or the project's writer; preserve exact approved wording. Build the copy deck with a stable slot ID, screen/state, purpose, text, language/direction, character range, tone note, source lines and all fact references. Every claim, statistic, named customer, logo and quotation needs a confirmed project fact and any required consent. Source presence alone is not confirmation. Record who checked it, when and against which source revision. A testimonial also needs its exact wording, speaker and permission.
    38	
    39	Use visible `[placeholder]` for a missing line, listing its slot, owner and required source. Never create a plausible headline, number or customer as temporary content. Placeholders may support an explicitly incomplete wireframe; they fail Step 9. Give the writer a text-fit request when a slot is too long; widen or reflow the layout first. Do not cut copy, ellipsize content or invent a shorter claim to fit a box.
    40	
    41	Collect image briefs describing the communication job, required content, rights and alt-text intent. Identify what not to show. Exit with an inventory that accounts for every slot, source, missing item and consent requirement. Known blocking content gaps remain visible.
    42	
    43	### 3. Information architecture
    44	
    45	Map each page and section to a visitor task and an exact brief line. Each section brief names its purpose, content slots, evidence, CTA and destination, expected state changes and success check. Remove sections with no job. A structural decision, grid break, illustration or motion proposal gets the same trace, so the critic can reject anything without one.
    46	
    47	Build a focused reference board of three real, inspected references: one useful for hierarchy, one for content density/type and one for the relevant interaction. These are comparison roles, not required vendors or aesthetic templates. Record URL, inspection date, local evidence where rights permit, what works, what would be wrong for this project and the relevant brief lines. Do not copy a reference's text, assets or trademark presentation. If a reference was inaccessible, mark it untested and replace it before the critic relies on it.
    48	
    49	Exit with the complete sitemap, task flows, section briefs and a single recommended direction. Reference selection must support the project's point of view, not impose a fashionable palette.
    50	
    51	### 4. Grayscale desktop wireframes
    52	
    53	Lay out the full desktop page/template set at 1440 CSS px using the real copy deck, clear reading order and numbered states. Explore two structural options for genuinely open sections unless the brief specifies otherwise; the separate three-to-five variant loop does not require duplicating every page. Keep all options grayscale and type-neutral at this phase.
    54	
    55	Annotate column spans, reading order, grouping, content-driven height, intended media job, interaction/state behavior and brief references. Check the project's other permitted desktop widths. A centered section means its principal heading, copy and CTA are compositionally centered as a unit; centered images or cells alone do not count. Permit at most one such section per page. Do not evade the count by replacing semantic sections with anonymous containers.
    56	
    57	Run the wireframe phase of the quality gate before Step 5. It checks structure, copy, provenance and placeholders; it records unresolved visual checks as deferred to Step 6, never as passed. No centered three-icon row, vague hero over three cards, arbitrary KPI row, icon soup or unsourced data survives structural review. A placeholder or missing decision is listed explicitly. Exit with the complete grayscale set and testable issues, not a polished home page alone.
    58	
    59	### 5. Test and approve the desktop wireframes
    60	
    61	Use the existing Step 5 gate as the explicit boundary before color and type work. No new approval gate is added. Test whether each audience can identify the purpose, find the relevant evidence and complete the primary task. Record tasks, actual participants or review method, observed failures and limitations; do not invent user research. The QA gate checks structure and accessibility intent. The writer checks copy and claim sources. The critic reviews structure and copy together against the brief and real reference work.
    62	
    63	Fix critical task failures and all structural/copy BLOCK findings, then obtain explicit approval for the complete scoped desktop wireframe set with version IDs. Approval of one page does not approve its siblings or templates. If unresolved copy or a consequential question prevents a meaningful structure decision, the gate stays On hold. This is where the no-AI-look checks run before styling; they run again on the final visual version.
    64	
    65	### 6. Desktop visual design
    66	
    67	Only after Step 5 approval, develop the chosen point of view with type, spacing, color, assets and motion. Start with the project's approved tokens and components. A new brand token still requires the existing approval. Pair display and reading roles deliberately; a single family with a justified role contrast is valid. Select fonts only after checking the project's language coverage, weight availability, licence and real text rendering.
    68	
    69	Use at most **six distinct rendered type sizes per page**: display, title, section heading, body, secondary text and label. Six supplies a useful hierarchy without arbitrary intermediate steps; fewer is preferable when sufficient. Document font family, fallback, size, line height, weight and tracking for each role. Weight and spacing carry distinctions before another size is added. The six-size ceiling applies within each locale; translations may use a separately approved metric-equivalent scale with the same ceiling. Text must remain readable at user zoom.
    70	
    71	Use a named spacing scale and specify the grid, margins, gutters, alignment lines and section rhythm in tokens. Every intentional grid break identifies the brief-backed reason and the reading-order consequence. Do not fabricate irregularity to appear human. Use neutrals and one strong accent; accent-bearing area must be at most ten percent in both the full-page composition and the reviewed desktop viewport. Semantic colors may communicate states but require text or shape as well; they are not extra decorative accents. The screenshot review catches color inside media and effects that DOM measurements miss.
    72	
    73	Avoid a default purple-blue combination, generic gradient hero, stock glass effects or repeated rounded-card grid. An existing brand conflict is reported for a decision, not silently rebranded. Each visual and icon must help someone understand, navigate, decide or recognize something. Remove it when that job cannot be named.
    74	
    75	For each motion, record the job, trigger, duration in milliseconds, easing, affected elements, interruption/cleanup behavior and static fallback. Under reduced motion there is no movement. Essential content remains visible with JavaScript disabled; the project's specified script-failure deadline is an acceptance check. Motion has no value merely because a page feels quiet without it.
    76	
    77	Render every required language and direction with actual writer-approved copy, then perform a clearly labelled synthetic long-text stress test at 150 percent of the slot's typical length. This synthetic text is test input, never publishable copy. Character ranges are planning guidance: if an approved translation exceeds one, revise the range and layout rather than truncate. Use logical start/end layout rules; check mixed numbers, punctuation, links and directional icons in RTL. Never mirror logos or text blindly. Exit with approved desktop visuals, complete state behavior and no blocking craft, copy or accessibility defects.
    78	
    79	### 7. Mobile and tablet
    80	
    81	This step is retained unchanged in the sequence. It may begin only after the approver approves desktop and the brief explicitly allows it. Until then its status is deferred, not passed, and no mobile/tablet layouts are made. For an approved desktop-only delivery, the brief must record that this step is outside the current scope; that record is not a mobile approval.
    82	
    83	When authorized, derive smaller layouts and navigation from the same copy, tasks and tokens. Document reflow and touch behavior without relying on hover. Test all key journeys and relevant states. The current rewrite does not authorize that phase.
    84	
    85	### 8. Validate, document and final design approval
     1	# Critic rubric — copy and design together
     2	
     3	The critic is independent and read-only. Load the frozen brief, rules, settings, copy/fact ledgers, reference board, scoped screen/state renders and integrated quality evidence. Do not start from the designer's self-assessment. Evaluate each page and all scoped states/locales against their real reference work; check a composition both at full-page scale and at readable 1440-pixel size. Missing evidence is FAIL, not benefit of the doubt.
     4	
     5	## Score anchors and pass rule
     6	
     7	Use zero-to-four scores only to make iteration visible: **0** absent or contradicted; **1** recognizable intent with major defects; **2** functional but generic or materially incomplete; **3** clear, specific and supported with all required checks passing; **4** unusually resolved craft, supported by concrete comparisons and detail. A score of four needs an explanation, not enthusiasm.
     8	
     9	Every row and every page receives a public **PASS or FAIL**, a score, an artifact reference and a reason. PASS requires at least 3 in every applicable row, all hard checks passing and no open P0/P1. Scores are never averaged to compensate for a failed dimension. A WARN can remain only with a named owner and explicit disposition and no unanswered build question.
    10	
    11	| Dimension | PASS at 3 requires | Evidence / automatic failure |
    12	|---|---|---|
    13	| Brief and point of view | One legible idea serving this audience/job, expressed consistently across the page | Brief lines for every section and deliberate decision; any orphan section fails |
    14	| Copy specificity and truth | Accurate approved wording, identifiable offering/task and confirmed proof | Slot/source/claim ledger, project-wording checks; transferable headline, invented proof or filler fails |
