| R14-GPT-02 | A | P2 | `release/tools/integrity.mjs:54` | **N12; new shadow-coverage gap.** Contrast scans only `document.querySelectorAll('body *')`. Executed mock reproduction: identical open-shadow text changes from readable to white-on-white without JavaScript; light-DOM heading coverage keeps both vectors identical and integrity **PASS**. Browser reproduction **UNVERIFIED**. | Traverse open shadow roots for contrast measurements and resolve backgrounds through slots and shadow hosts. Record unmeasurable rendered text as unknown. Add a declarative-shadow regression where only JavaScript-off contrast fails and require failed integrity. |
| R14-GPT-03 | A | P2 | `release/tools/width-screen.mjs:153` | **N16 incomplete.** `closedShadowRoots(page)` runs before the width loop; every width reuses that result. Executed adapter reproduction: a closed root present only at 320 yields **PASS**, with CDP queried only at 1440. Browser reproduction **UNVERIFIED**. | Count closed roots after each viewport change has settled, immediately before measuring that width. Fail closed separately at each width. Add a responsive regression creating a closed root at 320 and removing its host at 1440. |
| R14-GPT-04 | A | P2 | `release/tools/width-screen.mjs:33`, `release/tools/width-screen.mjs:74` | **New root-clipping gap.** The inspected list starts with `document.body`; vertical clipping checks only that list. Executed geometry mock: `html{height:100vh;overflow:hidden}` containing a naturally taller body reports no clipping at 1440 or 320 despite inaccessible lower text. Browser reproduction **UNVERIFIED**. | Measure root/viewport clipping and vertical content reachability, respecting body-overflow propagation and legitimate scrolling. Add browser regressions for a fixed-height hidden root with lower text and no usable internal scroller; require FAIL at 1440 and 320. |
| R14-GPT-05 | A | P2 | `release/tools/pre-handoff-check.mjs:213` | **N18; new shadow-motion gap.** Snapshots use only `document.querySelectorAll('body *')`. Executed in-memory `motionFor` reproduction: timer-driven shadow translation changes during its 700ms observation, while unchanged host geometry, zero rAF calls and zero WAAPI animations produce **PASS**. Browser reproduction **UNVERIFIED**. | Snapshot open-shadow descendants using a composed-tree walk and stable element identities. Add positional-page and declared-state regressions where `setInterval` moves shadow content during the observation window under reduced motion; require FAIL. |
| R14-GPT-06 | A | P2 | `scripts/package-r3.py:70` | **N10/q2 incomplete.** A `$value` node returns without inspecting metadata siblings; `walk_value` ignores arrays. Executed in-memory checks accept a token whose `$extensions.auth.password` contains a synthetic credential value; the content screen also misses it. Existing regressions cover direct dictionaries. | Recursively reject credential keys throughout every dictionary and list before validating token structure, including metadata, `$extensions` and composite `$value` arrays. Add package-input and changed-path regressions, retaining valid credential-free extension metadata. |
| R14-GPT-07 | B | P2 | `release/skills/design-research/SKILL.md:6`, `release/FANNAN-PLAN.md:31` | Research says “Research real reference work and techniques”; discovery inventories the brief without distinguishing observed user needs from assumptions. Visual references cannot establish workflow problems or priorities. | After Step 1’s first paragraph, add and link from `design-research`: “Maintain a discovery ledger for audience needs, current workflows, pain points and constraints. Record evidence source/date, assumptions, confidence, design consequence and risk. Use supplied or already-authorized research, support and analytics evidence. Assign material assumptions an owner and validation method in Step 5. Never present brief assertions or visual references as user research; preserve existing contact and payment approvals.” |
| R14-GPT-08 | B | P2 | `release/FANNAN-PLAN.md:49` | The IA exit requires “complete sitemap, task flows, section briefs” but gives no explicit navigation model or findability evaluation. Labels, hierarchy and current-location behavior can remain unspecified. | Before the Step 3 exit, add: “Specify navigation labels, grouping/order, destinations, current-location cues and back/escape behavior. Document discovery and movement between navigation levels. Specify search, filter and sort behavior when tasks require them; otherwise justify N/A. In Step 5, test finding priority destinations from realistic entry points using the proposed labels; record wrong turns and revisions.” |
| R14-GPT-09 | B | P2 | `release/references/handoff-contract.md:16`, `release/references/handoff-contract.md:37` | The repeated inventory requires “default, hover, focus, disabled, empty, loading and error”; success, selection and expansion states are not explicitly assessed. The validator’s required list also contains only those seven. | After the component-state guide, add and echo in `design-canvas`: “The seven baseline states are a minimum. Derive additional reachable states from actual interactions, including success/completion, selected/checked, expanded/collapsed, validation and read-only where applicable. Specify triggers, appearance, approved copy, semantics, announcements, keyboard/focus behavior and reference evidence. Success communicates the confirmed outcome and next action. The interaction critique fails when a reachable state or transition is unspecified.” |
| R14-GPT-10 | B | P2 | `release/references/handoff-contract.md:37` | “Appearance, behavior, entry/exit triggers, keyboard behavior and recovery” leaves core form decisions vague: validation timing, preserving input, retries, duplicate submission and destructive-action handling. | Add after the component-state guide: “For every form, specify labels, required/optional indicators, accepted formats, help, validation timing, inline/summary errors, failure focus, input preservation and correction steps. Define submitting, success, failure, retry and duplicate-submission behavior. For consequential actions, specify cancellation, confirmation or undo as appropriate, plus unsaved-change behavior. Each applicable behavior requires approved interface copy and a task-level acceptance check; justify N/A.” |
| R14-GPT-11 | B | P2 | `release/FANNAN-PLAN.md:61` | Step 5 records “actual participants or review method, observed failures and limitations” without defining neutral task execution, assistance recording or retesting. A guided demonstration could satisfy this wording. | After Step 5’s first paragraph, add: “Before testing, define each priority task’s neutral scenario, realistic starting point and observable completion criterion. Record participant/reviewer context, unaided completion, wrong turns, assistance and severity. Do not demonstrate the intended path first. Distinguish representative-user testing from expert walkthroughs and record remaining assumptions. Retest failed critical tasks after revisions against the new version. Resolve material uncertainty within the existing Step 5 approval gate.” |

